      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The differential diagnosis of benign-appearing posterior element lesions is complex."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To explore the etiology of an unusual benign lesion found on the lamina of L5 described in a case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Case report. The patient's medical, surgical and imaging histories as well as medical literature were reviewed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A 35-year old male complained of low back pain radiating to both legs for 5 months. Abdominal CT scan 1 year prior as well as post symptom-onset CT and MRI showed a well-corticated lesion lateral to the spinous process of L5. After failed rehabilitation and injection, operation revealed normal cortical bone. Pain was not markedly relieved post-operatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The pre-surgical differential diagnosis of this lesion included osteosarcoma and osteoid osteoma, however benign pathological results suggest the possibility of atypical spina bifida occulta, Baastrup changes, atypical sacralization of L5, or a resolved fracture with heterotopic ossification."
        }
      ]},
